{
  "source": "Long 2e Table 7.1, PDF pages 287-290 (printed pp. 283-286)",
  "about": "The hundred and two rows Table 7.1, \"Absorption Coefficients of Common Materials\", prints over four pages, in eleven groups headed in bold: walls, floors, ceilings, acoustical tile, glass cloth acoustical ceiling panels, seats and audience, duct liners, building insulation - fiberglass, fiberglass board (FB) and miscellaneous. Columns as printed: \"Material\", \"Mount\" and, under \"Frequency, Hz\", 125, 250, 500, 1k, 2k and 4k. The mount column prints A, E400 or F on sixty-two rows and nothing on the other forty, and the row holds it as the page prints it; the prose on printed page 281 (PDF page 285) says the mountings are those of ASTM C423 as drawn in Figure 7.21 on the next page, that \"mountings A, B, D, and E are used for most prefabricated products\", that \"mounting F is for duct liners\", and that \"the designation E-400, for example, indicates that mounting E was used and there was a 400 mm (16 in) airspace behind the test sample\". Fourteen rows are printed with a thickness alone for a name under a heading (the K-13 coating after its first row, the four duct liners, the four fiberglass insulations), and those are held with the heading as the name and the printed row as the variant. Fifteen finishes are printed twice, on an A mount and on an E400 or F mount, and are two rows here, keyed by the mount. A hundred rows are coefficients and two, under \"Miscellaneous\", are not: \"Musician (per person), with instrument\" prints six figures with no unit, held as an absorption area in sabins, square feet per person, for the reasons its note gives, and \"Air, Sabins per 1000 cubic feet @ 50% RH\" is an absorption per volume of air, filled in the three highest bands only; both are held as areas, converted to square metres and, for the air, to a cubic metre, and each converted cell says so. The book prints the table in inches, pounds per cubic foot and ounces per square yard, and the names keep that. No credit is printed for any row and no footnote. What the book says of the whole table is on printed pages 281 and 282 (PDF pages 285 and 286): the values are \"a representative sample of measured absorption data\", the list \"is by no means complete\", a diffuse-field coefficient \"is, in theory, always less than or equal to a value of one\" and \"values of alpha greater than 1 are sometimes measured\" by the reverberation time method, \"normally attributed to diffraction, the lack of a perfectly diffuse field in the measuring room, and edge conditions\", and \"when data are reported, the test mounting method must also be included since the airspace behind the material greatly affects the results\". Two cells of the plaster on brick and one of the concrete or terrazzo floor print three decimals where the rest of the table prints two.",
